Vitamin D Deficiency
Young Adult & Adolecence
Diffuse bone pain, Proximal muscle weakness, Bone tenderness on palpation, Low serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-050
Reason for Visit: Acute functional decline, severe proximal muscle weakness, and intractable diffuse bone pain resulting in an inability to safely mobilize or perform basic activities of daily living.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Housemate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a patient experiencing a progressive, severe generalized weakness culminating tonight in multiple falls while attempting to stand, accompanied by agonizing, diffuse lower back and pelvic pain. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critically required to safely rule out an acute spinal cord compression, severe myopathy, or pathological fracture, and to ensure the patient's immediate environmental safety prior to morning clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My bones feel like they are deeply bruised all over, and my thighs are so incredibly weak that my legs just gave out when I tried to stand up from the chair."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 4-month history of insidious, progressive generalized muscle weakness and profound fatigue. This has been accompanied by a diffuse, poorly localized bone pain that is consistently worst in the lower back, pelvis, and bilateral lower extremities. The acute crisis tonight was triggered by a severe worsening of proximal muscle myopathy, resulting in the patient repeatedly collapsing due to an inability to rise from a seated position without the legs giving way. The patient reports severe bone tenderness upon incidental contact. The flatmate notes the patient has exhibited a depressed mood and seasonal affective symptoms, spending the majority of the last 6 months indoors with zero sun exposure. The patient reports frequent muscle spasms and cramps in the calves.
Functional Status: Currently floor-bound after a non-traumatic collapse; unable to stand independently; utilizing a severe waddling gait when assisted.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme fall risk; functional incapacitation; living in a darkened environment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, chronically fatigued, and is wincing when shifting positions on the floor.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination reveals intact distal power, normal sensation, and normal reflexes, effectively ruling out acute radiculopathy or spinal cord compression. However, there is marked, symmetrical proximal muscle weakness (power 3/5 in bilateral quadriceps and hip flexors). The patient utilizes the Gowers' maneuver (climbing up their own legs) to stand when assisted. Musculoskeletal examination reveals exquisite, deep bone tenderness upon firm palpation of the sternum, bilateral anterior tibiae, and anterior pelvis, completely distinct from joint or muscle pain. The clinical phenotype strongly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, bone tenderness on palpation, and highly anticipates a severely depressed serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D level.
Environmental Observations: The apartment is heavily curtained, entirely blocking natural sunlight; the patient works permanent night shifts.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Symptomatic Vitamin D Deficiency (Suspected Osteomalacia) - Status: Acute functional decompensation
Differentials:

Polymyositis - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause due to the prominent presence of severe, distinct deep bone tenderness (sternum/tibia), which is a hallmark of osteomalacia, rather than pure painless muscle inflammation.

Spinal Cord Compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to the perfectly normal distal neurological examination, normal reflexes, intact sphincter tone, and the gradual, insidious 4-month onset of symptoms.

Fibromyalgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the objective presence of severe, verifiable proximal muscle weakness (inability to stand from a chair) and distinct bony tenderness, which point to a metabolic/structural defect rather than a pure central pain sensitization syndrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Severe fall risk, functional limitation)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe functional incapacitation driven by profound proximal myopathy and generalized bone pain, classic hallmarks of severe Vitamin D deficiency progressing to osteomalacia. The sudden inability to stand and repeated non-traumatic falls necessitated an immediate after-hours home visit to definitively exclude a catastrophic neurological event (cord compression) or an acute pathological fracture. Clinical examination safely confirmed the etiology as a profound metabolic myopathy and structural bone pain. Delaying assessment would have left the patient immobilized, terrified of a severe neurological disease, and at extreme risk of traumatic injury. The urgent visit provided crucial medical validation, safe mobilization strategies, and a structured diagnostic pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Assisted the patient safely back to bed. Provided direct clinical reassurance that the profound weakness is a reversible metabolic issue, not permanent nerve damage. Ordered an urgent fasting comprehensive metabolic panel (Calcium, Phosphate, ALP, PTH) and a serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D level via the regular GP to confirm the expected severe depletion (typically below 20 ng/mL) and secondary hyperparathyroidism.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent traumatic fractures while your bones are structurally weak, implement strict activity modification: do not attempt to stand up quickly or carry any heavy objects, utilizing the flatmate's physical support for all transfers over the next week. To naturally kickstart your body's vitamin synthesis, expose your bare forearms and face to direct, unprotected mid-day sunlight for exactly 15 minutes daily starting tomorrow, actively utilizing ultraviolet light to trigger the dermal production of Vitamin D.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review via telehealth within 48 hours to review the critical blood results and immediately initiate high-dose pharmacological Vitamin D replacement therapy (e.g., Cholecalciferol 50,000 IU weekly).

